55,3,1,talk,Asuka,,Option A is simple but might be too steep for newcomers. Option B has a history of causing friction. Option C risks further attendance drops.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,Option C is a treasurer's nightmare. Option B is a calculation headache. Option A is the only one that keeps the workload manageable.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Option A is unfair to those who rarely attend, but Option B might make the ""lower grade"" members feel like second-class citizens.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Option C is out; it turns the club into a series of transactions. Option A preserves the sense of membership, even if it's a bit blunt.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Takumi,,Option A is the only way to guarantee the fixed costs are covered upfront. It removes the guesswork and the chasing of payments.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Shunichi, I agree that the social atmosphere is fragile. Option A is the most invisible and least likely to create ""grades"" of members.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Jonathan, the ""unfairness"" of a flat fee is a small price to pay for a system that doesn't cause the committee to burn out.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, you make a fair point. If the alternative is a system that creates social friction or administrative collapse, A is the safest.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Takumi, if you can set a figure that covers the fixed costs without being exorbitant, I am fully behind Option A.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"I can do that. As long as we stop trying to be ""precise"" with every single event, we can set a stable annual fee.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"I vote for Option A. It avoids the friction of tiers and the barriers of activity-based charging, provided the fee is reasonable.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,I vote for Option A. The administrative efficiency is the priority here to ensure the club's long-term survival.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I vote for Option A. While not perfectly equitable, it is the most stable and least disruptive to the social fabric.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,I vote for Option A. We must prioritize the feeling of belonging over a transactional approach to our meetings.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Takumi,,I vote for Option A. It secures the budget and eliminates the uncertainty of our income.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
